@@ -0,0 +1,36 @@
+# Research
+
+## Inventory model and product response
+
+- Stock exposed by `GET /api/v2/product/{sku}` is `ProductAvailability.productQuantity`, mapped by `ReadableProductMapper` and `ReadableProductPopulator` into `ReadableProduct.quantity`.
+- Product-level merchant availability is `Product.available`; purchasability is also influenced by `ProductAvailability.productStatus`.
+- The v2 product mapping initializes `canBePurchased` from availability/quantity in `ReadableProductMapper`, but does not explicitly force `available` false when quantity reaches zero. The relevant code is `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/mapper/catalog/product/ReadableProductMapper.java`.
+
+## Checkout
+
+- Both checkout endpoints are in `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/api/v1/order/OrderApi.java`; they delegate to `OrderFacadeImpl.processOrder`.
+- The v1 checkout path builds order products from the persisted cart in `OrderFacadeImpl.processOrder(PersistableOrder, ...)`, then calls `OrderServiceImpl.processOrder`.
+- `OrderServiceImpl.process` currently decrements every availability on a product, but looks up `productService.getById(orderProduct.getId())`. `OrderProduct.id` is the order-product row ID, not the catalog product ID, so this can affect the wrong product or fail. It also decrements all availability rows, not the single stock represented by the API, and logs shortages instead of throwing.
+- Validation in `OrderFacadeImpl` only checks the default-region availability and occurs before the service decrement. It does not provide a single atomic validate-then-decrement operation for the whole cart.
+- Payment/order persistence and inventory mutation are in the same service method but no explicit transaction covers the whole checkout path. A failed inventory check after order creation could therefore leave partial state.
+
+## Cart changes
+
+- `ShoppingCartFacadeImpl` handles the requested endpoints. `addToCart` calls `readableShoppingCart`, `modifyCart` handles PUT, and `modifyCartMulti` handles POST `/multi`.
+- `createCartItem` rejects unavailable/zero-stock products, but only checks zero/nonzero and does not reject a requested quantity greater than stock. Its checks differ from the API cart path and inspect all availability rows.
+- `readableShoppingCart` increments an existing matching line item before saving, so the requested quantity must be checked against existing quantity plus the incoming quantity.
+- `modifyCart` creates/validates a new item before changing the persisted cart, but its current validation is not quantity-aware; it can set a quantity above stock.
+- `modifyCartMulti` calls `createCartItems` and then mutates each line item in sequence. It has no complete proposed-state validation before mutation, so multi-item changes need an explicit validate-first phase to guarantee all-or-none behavior.
+- Lowering/removing quantities should remain allowed even if current stock is lower, while additions/increases must respect stock.
+
+## Order status/cancellation
+
+- `PUT /api/v1/private/orders/{id}/status` is in `OrderApi`; it parses `OrderStatus` and calls `OrderFacadeImpl.updateOrderStatus`.
+- `updateOrderStatus` currently returns for the same status, otherwise changes status and records history. It has no stock restoration and permits transitions away from `CANCELED`.
+- `OrderProduct` stores the ordered catalog product reference/ID and quantity; restoration must use that catalog product and be idempotent for already-canceled orders.
+- A 409 response is not currently represented by the generic service exception handler (which maps `ServiceRuntimeException` to 400), so a dedicated conflict exception/handler or `ResponseStatusException` path is required for cart, checkout, and invalid status transitions.
+
+## Tests and affected areas
+
+- Existing integration coverage is under `sm-shop/src/test/java/com/salesmanager/test/shop/integration/cart` and common product helpers under `sm-shop/src/test/java/com/salesmanager/test/shop/common`.
+- The implementation affects `sm-shop` cart facade/API, checkout/order facade/service, product v2 mapping, exception-to-HTTP mapping, and likely integration tests. Inventory entity/service APIs can be reused; no new stock field is needed.
